    "text": "{\n  \"anomaly_detected\": true,\n  \"confidence\": 0.5,\n  \"anomalies\": [\n    {\n      \"category\": \"bridged_nets\",\n      \"components\": [\"U2\", \"U3\", \"R4\", \"R6\", \"D1\", \"J4\"],\n      \"pins\": [\"U2.3\", \"U2.7\", \"U3.3\", \"U3.7\", \"R4.1\", \"J4.3\"],\n      \"nets\": [\"SCK\"],\n      \"observed\": \"The SCK net joins PB4 (U2.3 XTAL2/PB4, U3.3 PB4) and PB2 (U2.7 PB2, U3.7 PB2) together with the ISP clock pin J4.3 and the tach series resistor R4.1. Every other MCU pin-to-net mapping is one-to-one (RST=PB5, PWM=PB1, SDA=PB0, POT=PB3), so PB4 and PB2 are not independent nets.\",\n      \"expected\": \"PB2 is the ISP SCK function (J4.3 should land on PB2 only). The /TACH_IN path through R4 should terminate on its own PB4 node, separate from SCK. SCK should not be shared with the tach input.\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"SCK : D1.1(K,passive) J1.3(Pin_3,passive) J4.3(SCK,passive) R4.1(passive) R6.2(passive) U2.3(XTAL2/PB4,bidirectional) U2.7(PB2,bidirectional) U3.3(PB4,bidirectional) U3.7(PB2,bidirectional)\"},\n        {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"/TACH_IN : J2.3(Pin_3,passive) R4.2(passive)\"},\n        {\"source\": \"spice\", \"quote\": \"R4 SCK /TACH_IN 1k\"}\n      ],\n      \"impact\": \"The fan tach signal is loaded and clamped by the SCK/PB2 line, and the ISP clock and tach input share one node. ISP programming can be disrupted, and tach pulses would be corrupted or masked by the programming and clock activity. The tach input on PB4 is likely unreadable as intended.\",\n      \"confidence\": 0.5\n    }\n  ],\n  \"summary\": \"The SCK net merges ISP clock PB2 with PB4 and the fan tach series resistor R4, which the one-to-one pin mapping elsewhere in the design does not support. The tach input and ISP clock appear shorted together. The specification describes an unrelated repository, so this verdict rests on standard practice and the netlist.\"\n}",
